            {
              "id": "1d03ed76-770b-3336-bb80-3299283993ee",
              "name": "Subprocess Called With Shell=True Can Lead To Command Injection",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThis is an OS command injection risk. In Python, calling subprocess.Popen with shell=True causes the command string to be interpreted by a system shell, so any untrusted data embedded in that string can change the command that actually runs.\n\nIn the shown sink, passing command to subprocess.Popen(..., shell=True) allows shell metacharacters such as ;, &&, |, $, or backticks to be processed. If command is influenced by external input, an attacker can execute arbitrary operating system commands with the privileges of the Python process.\n\nThe fix is to avoid the shell entirely and pass the executable and each argument as a list with shell=False. If dynamic input is required, validate it against a strict allowlist and keep it as a single argument value rather than concatenating it into a shell command string.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not build shell command strings from variables or user input. In Python, prefer subprocess.run or subprocess.Popen with shell=False and provide arguments as a sequence so the interpreter does not invoke a shell. Use absolute executable paths where practical.\n\nIf input must influence behavior, restrict it to a small allowlist of permitted commands, flags, or values. Validate each field separately according to expected format, length, and character set. Avoid passing user-controlled data into redirection, pipes, globbing, variable expansion, or command substitution contexts.\n\nIf shell usage is unavoidable, redesign the interface first. Escaping with shlex.quote is less safe than removing shell=True and should not be the primary defense for tainted input. Run child processes with the least privileges needed and handle errors explicitly.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport subprocess\n\nallowed_commands = {\"status\": [\"/usr/bin/mytool\", \"status\"], \"version\": [\"/usr/bin/mytool\", \"version\"]}\nuser_action = user_action.strip()\n\nif user_action not in allowed_commands:\n    raise ValueError(\"invalid command\")\n\nprocess = subprocess.Popen(\n    allowed_commands[user_action],\n    stdout=subprocess.PIPE,\n    stderr=subprocess.STDOUT,\n    shell=False,\n    text=True,\n)\noutput, _ = process.communicate()\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nsubprocess (Python standard library)\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Command Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Command_Injection)\n- [CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)\n- [Python subprocess module documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/subprocess.html)\n- [Prevent Command Injection in Python Subprocess Usage](https://securecodingpractices.com/prevent-command-injection-python-subprocess/)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-78",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-78"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "1d9e913f-c9ee-38b8-b1f2-e294f9bd4b79",
              "name": "Missing Function Level Access Control",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThis is a broken function-level access control issue. The view processes request data, resolves a trigger function, renders a template, and returns a response without enforcing authentication or checking whether the caller is allowed to use the selected action.\n\nIn Python web applications, a view should not rely on routing, obscurity, or client behavior to protect sensitive functionality. In the shown sink, any caller that can reach the endpoint may be able to invoke trigger-dependent behavior and receive a response, which can expose privileged actions or internal test functionality to unauthorized users.\n\nProtect the view at entry by requiring an authenticated user and then enforcing an explicit authorization decision before calling the trigger function or rendering sensitive content. Keep the authorization check server-side and fail closed with an HTTP 403 response when the user lacks permission.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nRequire authentication on the view with framework-provided decorators or middleware, then enforce object-level or action-level authorization before executing any trigger-dependent logic. Check permissions on the server for every request, not just in the UI or client code. If access is denied, return 403 and do not continue processing.\n\nApply least privilege by defining narrow permissions for viewing the page and for executing the trigger action. Avoid exposing internal or diagnostic handlers through a public endpoint. If different triggers have different sensitivity levels, map each trigger to an explicit permission and validate that mapping before invocation. Log denied access attempts and review routes periodically to ensure all sensitive views are protected consistently.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom django.contrib.auth.decorators import login_required, permission_required\nfrom django.http import HttpResponseForbidden\nfrom django.template.loader import get_template\nfrom django.utils.html import escape\nfrom rest_framework.response import Response\n\n@login_required\n@permission_required('app.can_use_trigger_view', raise_exception=True)\ndef _view(request):\n    user_input = _get_user_input(request)\n    trigger_func = get_trigger(name, trigger)\n\n    if trigger_func is not None:\n        if not request.user.has_perm('app.can_execute_trigger'):\n            return HttpResponseForbidden('Forbidden')\n        trigger_func(user_input)\n\n    template = get_template(\"{}.html\".format(name)).render({}, request)\n\n    if name == \"xss\" and trigger == \"raw\":\n        template += \"<p>XSS: \" + escape(user_input) + \"</p>\"\n\n    return Response(template)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nDjango\ndjangorestframework\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10: Broken Access Control](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [Django authentication in views](https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/stable/topics/auth/default/)\n- [Django login_required decorator](https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/stable/topics/auth/default/#the-login-required-decorator)\n- [CWE-306: Missing Authentication for Critical Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html)\n- [CWE-862: Missing Authorization](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-306",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-862",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-306",
                  "CWE-862"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "21a13197-c75e-3a53-a9f5-8e6b5a67e488",
              "name": "Direct Use Of Jinja2 May Lead To XSS",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-site scripting risk from unsafe template environment configuration. Creating a Jinja2 Environment directly in Python can disable or bypass automatic HTML escaping if it is not configured explicitly for HTML output. When untrusted data is rendered into templates without autoescaping, attacker-controlled input can be interpreted as markup or script in the browser.\n\nFor this sink, the issue is the direct construction of Environment(loader=loader) without enabling selective autoescaping. In Flask applications, the safer pattern is to use render_template(), which uses Flask's configured Jinja environment and enables escaping for HTML templates by default. If a custom Jinja environment is required, configure autoescape with select_autoescape for HTML-like template types so rendered variables are escaped consistently.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not create a bare Jinja2 Environment for HTML rendering. Prefer Flask's render_template() with .html templates so automatic escaping is applied by default. If custom environment setup is necessary, create it with autoescape=select_autoescape(['html', 'htm', 'xml']) and keep autoescape enabled for all browser-rendered content. Avoid marking untrusted input as safe, and only use the safe filter or Markup objects for content that has already been sanitized for its exact output context. Keep templates separate from code, use context-appropriate escaping, and validate or sanitize rich user content before rendering it.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom flask import render_template\n\n# Prefer Flask's built-in rendering for HTML responses.\ndef show_page(user_name: str):\n    return render_template(\"profile.html\", user_name=user_name)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nFlask\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Cross Site Scripting (XSS)](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/xss/)\n- [Flask Templating Documentation](https://flask.palletsprojects.com/en/stable/templating/)\n- [Jinja API Documentation](https://jinja.palletsprojects.com/en/stable/api/)\n- [Jinja Template Designer Documentation - HTML Escaping](https://jinja.palletsprojects.com/en/stable/templates/#html-escaping)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "280408b5-0c70-3558-ad51-586dc0bf2c45",
              "name": "Vulnerable XML Parsing With Untrusted Data",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnsafe XML parsing of untrusted input can expose Python applications to XML external entity expansion, entity-based denial of service, and related parser attacks. In this case, xml.dom.pulldom.parseString is called directly on user-controlled data, so crafted XML may trigger dangerous parser behavior before the application processes the document.\n\nFor this sink, replace the standard-library parser call with the hardened defusedxml equivalent so hostile XML features are blocked by default. If the application must keep using standard-library XML modules, initialize defusedxml.defuse_stdlib() before any XML parsing occurs so the vulnerable parser behavior is disabled process-wide.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not parse untrusted XML with Python standard-library XML parsers unless they have been explicitly hardened. Prefer defusedxml wrappers for all XML entry points, including parseString, parse, and DOM-style helpers. Apply the hardened parser consistently anywhere request data, uploaded files, message bodies, or third-party feeds are converted from XML.\n\nAlso limit accepted XML size, reject unnecessary DTD and entity usage, and use simpler data formats such as JSON when XML-specific features are not required. If standard-library compatibility is needed across a larger codebase, call defusedxml.defuse_stdlib() during application startup before any XML module is imported or used.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom defusedxml import pulldom\n\ndef parse_user_xml(user_input: str):\n    return pulldom.parseString(user_input)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\ndefusedxml>=0.7.1\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: XML External Entity (XXE) Processing](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/XML_External_Entity_(XXE)_Processing)\n- [CWE-611: Improper Restriction of XML External Entity Reference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/611.html)\n- [Python Documentation: XML security vulnerabilities](https://docs.python.org/3/library/xml.html#xml-vulnerabilities)\n- [defusedxml on PyPI](https://pypi.org/project/defusedxml/)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-611",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/611.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-611"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "29e0f4ac-9d48-38b4-ba48-b51dc9478d2c",
              "name": "Dangerous Use Of Exec In Dynamic Content Evaluation",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nDynamic code execution via exec(). In Python, exec() parses and runs Python source at runtime, so passing data that can be influenced by users, files, environment variables, network responses, or other external sources can allow arbitrary code execution.\n\nFor the sink shown as exec(code), the safest fix is to remove dynamic execution and replace it with explicit control flow, such as a dispatch table of allowed operations or direct function calls. If behavior must remain configurable, map trusted identifiers to predefined callables instead of executing source text.\n\nIf complete removal is not immediately possible, ensure the executed content is not externally definable, constrain it to a strict allowlist of known values, and avoid exposing application globals, locals, or powerful builtins to the execution context. This reduces risk, but replacing exec() is the preferred remediation.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not execute dynamically constructed Python code. Replace exec() with explicit program logic, a dictionary that maps approved command names to functions, or structured configuration parsed as data rather than code. Keep the set of allowed operations small and fixed.\n\nWhere input affects behavior, validate it against a strict allowlist and reject anything unexpected. Prefer simple identifiers over expressions. If legacy code still relies on exec(), isolate it, pass empty or tightly restricted globals and locals, remove access to builtins where possible, and treat this only as a temporary containment step rather than a full fix.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nALLOWED_ACTIONS = {\n    \"start\": lambda: Package.Class.start(),\n    \"stop\": lambda: Package.Class.stop(),\n    \"reload\": lambda: Package.Class.reload(),\n}\n\naction = str(code).strip()\nhandler = ALLOWED_ACTIONS.get(action)\nif handler is None:\n    raise ValueError(\"Unsupported action\")\n\nhandler()\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nN/A\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Code Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Code_Injection)\n- [CWE-94: Improper Control of Generation of Code ('Code Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html)\n- [Python Documentation: exec()](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#exec)\n- [OWASP Input Validation Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Input_Validation_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-94",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-94"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "2c4feb15-e318-3ee5-a0a9-e096a4a70c84",
              "name": "Starting A Process With A Shell",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nOS command injection in Python process execution. Using os.system(cmd) starts a shell to interpret the command string, so any untrusted data concatenated into cmd can change the command being run and execute unintended shell metacharacters, pipelines, redirections, or additional commands.\n\nIn Python, the safer pattern is to avoid the shell entirely and execute the target program with subprocess.run using a list of arguments and shell=False. This prevents shell parsing and keeps user input as data instead of executable syntax. If only a fixed operation is needed, validate input against a strict allowlist before passing it as an argument.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nReplace os.system with subprocess.run or another subprocess API that accepts an argument list, and keep shell=False. Do not build commands with string concatenation, interpolation, or format strings when any part may come from users, files, environment variables, or external systems. Prefer fixed executable paths and fixed flags.\n\nWhen input must influence behavior, restrict it to an allowlist of known-safe values or validate it against a narrow expected format before use. If invoking a shell is unavoidable, redesign the code if possible; otherwise apply strict input validation and escaping with extreme care, understanding that escaping is more error-prone than avoiding the shell entirely.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport subprocess\n\nuser_value = user_value.strip()\nallowed_values = {\"start\", \"stop\", \"status\"}\nif user_value not in allowed_values:\n    raise ValueError(\"invalid command option\")\n\nsubprocess.run([\"/usr/bin/mytool\", user_value], check=True, shell=False)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nsubprocess (Python standard library)\nshlex (Python standard library, only if parsing fixed trusted command fragments is required)\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Command Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Command_Injection)\n- [CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)\n- [Python subprocess — Subprocess management](https://docs.python.org/3/library/subprocess.html)\n- [Python shlex — Simple lexical analysis](https://docs.python.org/3/library/shlex.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-78",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-78"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "2fed86ac-adca-3d48-8012-53f291ab291e",
              "name": "Missing Function Level Access Control",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThis is a missing function-level access control issue. The view executes sensitive behavior based on request-controlled values, but it is not protected by authentication or authorization checks before invoking the selected trigger function or returning content.\n\nIn Python web applications, a view should enforce access policy at the entry point so unauthenticated or unauthorized users cannot reach privileged code paths. In the shown sink, the request can influence which trigger is resolved and executed, and the response may include unsafe content. Protect the view with authentication and explicit permission checks before processing input, dispatching to a function, or rendering a response.\n\nApply access control directly on the view using Django decorators such as login_required and permission_required, or perform equivalent checks inside the function. Also restrict the set of allowed trigger names to a server-defined allowlist so that only intended actions are reachable after authorization succeeds.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nRequire authentication for the entire view and enforce an explicit authorization rule that matches the action being performed. In Django, prefer login_required together with permission_required, user_passes_test, or object-level authorization checks when access depends on the specific resource.\n\nDo not rely on hidden routes, client-side checks, or template naming conventions as access control. Validate request parameters against a strict server-side allowlist before selecting code paths, templates, or actions. Keep privileged operations behind narrowly scoped permissions, deny by default, and return 403 for authenticated users without access.\n\nAs a defense-in-depth measure, avoid dynamic function dispatch from user-controlled values unless the mapping is fixed and reviewed. Render templates through Django's template system with context variables instead of concatenating untrusted input into HTML responses.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom django.contrib.auth.decorators import login_required, permission_required\nfrom django.http import HttpResponse, HttpResponseBadRequest\nfrom django.shortcuts import render\n\nALLOWED_TEMPLATES = {\"home\", \"help\", \"status\"}\nALLOWED_TRIGGERS = {\n    \"safe\": safe_trigger,\n}\n\n@login_required\n@permission_required(\"app.view_trigger_page\", raise_exception=True)\ndef _view(request, name, trigger):\n    user_input = _get_user_input(request)\n\n    if name not in ALLOWED_TEMPLATES:\n        return HttpResponseBadRequest(\"Invalid template\")\n\n    trigger_func = ALLOWED_TRIGGERS.get(trigger)\n    if trigger_func is None:\n        return HttpResponseBadRequest(\"Invalid trigger\")\n\n    trigger_func(user_input)\n\n    context = {\"user_input\": user_input}\n    return render(request, f\"{name}.html\", context)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nfrom django.contrib.auth.decorators import login_required, permission_required\nfrom django.http import HttpResponseBadRequest\nfrom django.shortcuts import render\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Broken Access Control](https://owasp.org/www-community/Broken_Access_Control)\n- [OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [Django authentication system](https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/stable/topics/auth/default/)\n- [Django login_required decorator](https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/stable/topics/auth/default/#the-login-required-decorator)\n- [Django permission_required decorator](https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/stable/topics/auth/default/#the-permission-required-decorator)\n- [CWE-862: Missing Authorization](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-862",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-862"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "393820c2-e77a-3b0a-babb-393ca29206ea",
              "name": "Missing Function Level Access Control",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nBroken access control at the function level. In this Python handler flow, a helper reads user-controlled data from request.rel_url.query without any surrounding authentication or authorization enforcement on the route that calls it. If the endpoint is reachable by unauthenticated or under-privileged users, they can invoke application behavior that should be restricted.\n\nReading a query parameter is not inherently dangerous, but when the containing view lacks access checks, any downstream logic that uses the returned value can be exposed to unauthorized callers. In aiohttp-style applications, the fix is to enforce authentication before the handler runs and perform an explicit authorization check inside the handler for the required role or permission before processing request parameters.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nRequire authentication on every route that should not be public, and enforce authorization in the handler or middleware based on roles, permissions, or ownership. Do not rely on hidden URLs, client-side checks, or the presence of request parameters to protect sensitive functionality.\n\nFor Python web applications, centralize identity loading in middleware, attach the authenticated user to the request, and fail closed when user context is missing. Apply route-level guards consistently, then validate that the authenticated principal is allowed to perform the specific action before reading and acting on user-supplied input.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom aiohttp import web\n\n\ndef require_authenticated(handler):\n    async def wrapped(request):\n        user = request.get(\"user\")\n        if user is None:\n            raise web.HTTPUnauthorized(text=\"Authentication required\")\n        return await handler(request)\n    return wrapped\n\n\ndef require_role(role_name):\n    def decorator(handler):\n        async def wrapped(request):\n            user = request.get(\"user\")\n            if user is None:\n                raise web.HTTPUnauthorized(text=\"Authentication required\")\n            roles = user.get(\"roles\", [])\n            if role_name not in roles:\n                raise web.HTTPForbidden(text=\"Insufficient permissions\")\n            return await handler(request)\n        return wrapped\n    return decorator\n\n\ndef _get_user_input(request):\n    return request.rel_url.query.get(\"user_input\", \"\")\n\n\n@require_authenticated\n@require_role(\"admin\")\nasync def protected_view(request):\n    user_input = _get_user_input(request)\n    return web.json_response({\"user_input\": user_input})\n\n\napp = web.Application()\napp.router.add_get(\"/protected\", protected_view)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\naiohttp\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Broken Access Control](https://owasp.org/www-community/Broken_Access_Control)\n- [OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-285: Improper Authorization](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/285.html)\n- [aiohttp Web Server Quickstart](https://docs.aiohttp.org/en/stable/web_quickstart.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-285",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/285.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-285"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "3d4f9bb3-2a26-38a0-b83a-4866c9b12dd2",
              "name": "Missing Function Level Access Control",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThis issue is missing function-level access control. The view handles an HTTP request and returns application content without enforcing authentication or checking whether the caller is allowed to access the resource.\n\nIn Python web applications, access control should be applied at the view boundary before sensitive logic or template rendering occurs. An async view like this can be invoked directly unless it is protected by authentication and permission checks. If the rendered page is intended for authenticated users or a restricted role, an unauthenticated or unauthorized client may be able to retrieve it.\n\nProtect the handler with explicit authentication and authorization checks that are compatible with async views. Deny access by default, verify the user identity from the request, and enforce the specific permission or role required for this endpoint before returning the response.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nRequire authentication on every non-public view and perform authorization checks inside or at the decorator layer before any response content is generated. For Django, use login_required for authenticated access and permission_required or a custom role check for resource-specific authorization. Keep public endpoints explicitly documented instead of relying on omission.\n\nAvoid dynamically selecting templates or other protected resources from unchecked state when serving restricted pages. Use fixed route-to-template mappings, enforce least privilege with narrowly scoped permissions, and return 403 for authenticated users who lack the required permission. Apply the same policy consistently to async and sync views.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom django.contrib.auth.decorators import login_required, permission_required\nfrom django.http import HttpResponse\nfrom django.template.loader import get_template\n\n@login_required\n@permission_required(\"app.view_root\", raise_exception=True)\nasync def _root(request):\n    template = get_template(\"root.html\")\n    return HttpResponse(template.render({}, request))\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nDjango>=4.2\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Broken Access Control](https://owasp.org/www-community/Broken_Access_Control)\n- [OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-306: Missing Authentication for Critical Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html)\n- [CWE-862: Missing Authorization](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html)\n- [Django authentication and authorization](https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/stable/topics/auth/default/)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-306",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-862",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-306",
                  "CWE-862"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "43440521-d319-3495-b4ba-129bc01aea86",
              "name": "Dangerous Use Of Eval In Dynamic Content",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnsafe dynamic code execution. In Python, calling eval() on a string causes the interpreter to execute that string as Python code. When the evaluated value can be influenced by external input, an attacker can run arbitrary expressions, access application data, or trigger system-level behavior through imported modules and built-in functions.\n\nThe sink shown as return eval(code) is especially risky because it directly returns the result of executing untrusted content. The safest fix is to remove eval() entirely and replace it with a parser or explicit allowlist based on the expected input format. If the code only needs to read Python literals such as numbers, strings, lists, or dictionaries, use ast.literal_eval() instead of executing arbitrary expressions.\n\nIf the input is not meant to be Python syntax, parse it with a format-specific library such as json.loads() or convert supported operations through explicit application logic. Avoid trying to secure eval() with string filtering, because blacklist-based checks are easy to bypass in Python.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not use eval() on data that may come from users, files, environment variables, network responses, or other untrusted sources. Replace dynamic execution with safe parsing and explicit logic. For structured data, prefer json.loads() or another format parser. For Python literal values, use ast.literal_eval(), which accepts only basic literal structures and rejects arbitrary code execution. If only a small set of operations is required, implement an allowlist of supported commands and dispatch them directly instead of interpreting source text.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport ast\n\ndef execute_code(code: str):\n    return ast.literal_eval(code)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nimport ast\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Code Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Code_Injection)\n- [CWE-94: Improper Control of Generation of Code](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html)\n- [Python Documentation: eval()](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#eval)\n- [Python Documentation: ast.literal_eval](https://docs.python.org/3/library/ast.html#ast.literal_eval)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-94",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-94"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "46eff04f-ea3a-31d9-98c7-b4aab4183b34",
              "name": "Starting A Process With A Shell",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCommand injection through shell process execution. In Python, calling os.system(command) passes a string to the operating system shell, so any untrusted data embedded in command can change how the shell interprets and executes it.\n\nThis is dangerous because shell metacharacters, command chaining, variable expansion, redirection, and subshell syntax can all be abused to run unintended commands. For this sink, the fix is to avoid the shell entirely and execute a fixed program with an explicit argument list.\n\nUse subprocess.run with shell=False and provide arguments as a sequence instead of building a shell command string. If only a small set of commands is allowed, map user input to predefined values and reject anything else before execution.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not pass untrusted input to os.system, subprocess with shell=True, or any API that invokes a shell command string. Prefer subprocess.run or subprocess.Popen with shell=False and a list of arguments so the runtime does not perform shell parsing. Keep the executable path fixed, allowlist any user-selectable options, and validate each argument according to its expected type and format.\n\nIf shell usage is unavoidable, strictly separate trusted command templates from untrusted data and validate the data against a narrow allowlist before use. Quoting functions such as shlex.quote reduce parsing risk but are weaker than avoiding the shell altogether and should not be the primary defense for security-sensitive code.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport subprocess\n\ndef run_command(user_arg: str) -> int:\n    allowed_commands = {\n        \"status\": [\"/usr/bin/mytool\", \"status\"],\n        \"list\": [\"/usr/bin/mytool\", \"list\"],\n    }\n\n    if user_arg not in allowed_commands:\n        raise ValueError(\"invalid command\")\n\n    result = subprocess.run(\n        allowed_commands[user_arg],\n        check=False,\n        shell=False,\n        capture_output=True,\n        text=True,\n    )\n    return result.returncode\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nimport subprocess\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Command Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Command_Injection)\n- [CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)\n- [Python subprocess module documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/subprocess.html)\n- [Python shlex documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/shlex.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-78",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-78"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "47c44dac-29f9-3615-bc70-7019690c6759",
              "name": "Subprocess Call With Shell=True Is Dangerous",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nOS command injection risk through shell invocation. In Python, calling subprocess.Popen with shell=True causes the command to be executed by a shell, which interprets metacharacters, expansions, environment settings, and command chaining syntax before launching the program.\n\nWhen the command string contains untrusted or partially trusted input, an attacker can alter its meaning and execute unintended commands. Even if the current input is expected to be safe, shell execution increases exposure by depending on shell parsing behavior instead of passing arguments directly to the target process.\n\nFor this sink, construct the command as a list of explicit arguments and call subprocess.Popen with shell=False. This bypasses shell parsing, preserves the intended argument boundaries, and keeps stdout and stderr capture behavior without invoking an intermediate shell.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid shell=True for process execution in Python. Pass the executable and each argument as separate list elements so the operating system invokes the program directly without shell interpretation. If any part of the command comes from a user, validate it against a strict allowlist of expected values or formats before using it.\n\nUse absolute paths for executables when practical, keep environment variables controlled, and prefer high-level APIs that do not require shell syntax. If shell behavior is truly unavoidable, do not concatenate input into the command string; instead redesign the call to remove shell features or isolate the operation behind a fixed wrapper with narrowly constrained inputs.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport subprocess\n\nprocess = subprocess.Popen(\n    [\"/path/to/program\", \"--option\", command],\n    stdout=subprocess.PIPE,\n    stderr=subprocess.STDOUT,\n    shell=False,\n    text=True,\n)\noutput, _ = process.communicate()\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nimport subprocess\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Command Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Command_Injection)\n- [OWASP OS Command Injection Defense Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/OS_Command_Injection_Defense_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)\n- [Python subprocess documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/subprocess.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-78",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-78"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "48147daf-7c72-341e-856d-1d9adb126066",
              "name": "Dynamic Code Execution Detected",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nDynamic code execution vulnerability. In Python, calling exec(code) executes the supplied string as Python statements, which allows attackers to run arbitrary code if any part of that value is influenced by untrusted input.\n\nThis is especially dangerous because exec can access imports, file operations, subprocess creation, and application objects in scope. In this case, the safest remediation is to remove exec entirely and replace it with explicit, allowlisted behavior that maps approved commands to real functions.\n\nIf the intent is to parse simple data rather than execute statements, use a safe parser such as ast.literal_eval for Python literals or json.loads for JSON. If dynamic behavior is required, restrict input to a fixed set of supported operations and dispatch through normal function calls instead of interpreting code text.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not pass untrusted or variable application data into exec or eval. Replace dynamic execution with direct function calls, a dispatch table, or another allowlist-based design where only predefined actions are possible. Validate input against a strict set of accepted values before selecting behavior.\n\nWhen the input represents data, parse it as data with the narrowest parser that matches the format, such as json.loads for JSON or ast.literal_eval only for trusted Python literal structures. Avoid trying to sandbox exec with custom globals or locals as a primary defense; removing the interpreter behavior is the reliable fix.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom typing import Any, Callable\n\n\ndef run_print() -> None:\n    print(\"approved action\")\n\n\ndef run_status() -> None:\n    print({\"status\": \"ok\"})\n\n\nALLOWED_ACTIONS: dict[str, Callable[[], None]] = {\n    \"print\": run_print,\n    \"status\": run_status,\n}\n\n\ndef execute_action(action: str) -> None:\n    handler = ALLOWED_ACTIONS.get(action)\n    if handler is None:\n        raise ValueError(\"Unsupported action\")\n    handler()\n\n\nexecute_action(code)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nfrom typing import Any, Callable\nfrom ast import literal_eval\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Code Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Code_Injection)\n- [CWE-94: Improper Control of Generation of Code ('Code Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html)\n- [Python Documentation: exec()](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#exec)\n- [Python Documentation: ast.literal_eval](https://docs.python.org/3/library/ast.html#ast.literal_eval)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-94",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-94"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "4991315d-4018-3a1b-8c91-4926c6f27649",
              "name": "Direct Use Of Jinja2 May Lead To XSS",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThis is a server-side template rendering XSS risk. In Python web applications, rendering a Jinja2 template directly can bypass the framework defaults that enable safe HTML autoescaping, especially when untrusted data such as request parameters is passed into the template context.\n\nIn the shown sink, user-controlled input from request.args is sent straight into template.render(...). If the template is created or loaded without HTML autoescaping, attacker-supplied markup or script can be injected into the response and executed in the browser. In Flask, the safer pattern is to render HTML views through render_template() with standard .html templates so Jinja autoescaping is applied consistently.\n\nUse Flask's template rendering for HTML responses, keep autoescaping enabled, and only mark content safe when it has been explicitly sanitized for the intended output context. Avoid constructing ad hoc Jinja environments for HTML unless autoescape is configured with select_autoescape for html, htm, and xml files.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nRender HTML responses with Flask's render_template() instead of calling Jinja Template.render() directly for request-driven pages. Store templates as .html files under the application's templates directory so autoescaping is applied by default. Treat all request data as untrusted, and do not use the safe filter, Markup, or disabled autoescape unless the content has been sanitized for HTML output. If a custom Jinja environment is required, configure autoescape explicitly with jinja2.select_autoescape for HTML-capable extensions and review templates for any raw output patterns.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom flask import request, render_template\n\nclass Package:\n    class Class:\n        def handler(self):\n            user_input = request.args.get(\"user_input\", \"\")\n            return render_template(\n                \"template-xss.html\",\n                path=\"/template-xss/jinja\",\n                user_input=user_input,\n            )\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nFlask\nJinja2\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Cross Site Scripting (XSS)](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/xss/)\n- [Flask Templating Documentation](https://flask.palletsprojects.com/en/stable/templating/)\n- [Jinja Template Designer Documentation - HTML Escaping](https://jinja.palletsprojects.com/en/stable/templates/#html-escaping)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "4cd1467d-b799-3d52-a087-5378ededda28",
              "name": "Missing Function Level Access Control",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nFunction-level access control is missing on this view. In Python web applications, a request handler that returns a response without first enforcing authentication and authorization can expose pages or actions to anonymous users or to users who should not have access.\n\nIn the shown sink, the view directly renders and returns a template response with no access check. For Django, protect the view with authentication such as login_required and add an explicit authorization check appropriate to the resource, such as permission_required or a manual user.has_perm test. This ensures only authenticated users with the required permission can reach the code path and receive the rendered page.\n\nAccess control should be enforced server-side for every entry point, even if navigation to the page is hidden in the UI. Relying on template names, routes, or client behavior does not prevent direct requests. Apply the same rule consistently across function-based views, class-based views, and any related API endpoints.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nRequire authentication and authorization on every view that serves protected content or performs sensitive actions. In Django, use login_required for authenticated access and permission_required, user_passes_test, or object-level authorization logic for role and resource checks. Deny by default and raise 403 for authenticated users who lack permission.\n\nCentralize access rules where possible so new routes are protected consistently. For class-based views, use LoginRequiredMixin and PermissionRequiredMixin. Review URL patterns, administrative pages, AJAX endpoints, and file or template rendering routes to ensure each one performs server-side checks before returning data or content. Avoid assuming that hidden links, obscure URLs, or client-side controls provide any protection.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom django.contrib.auth.decorators import login_required, permission_required\nfrom django.http import HttpResponse\nfrom django.template.loader import get_template\n\n@login_required\n@permission_required('app.view_root_page', raise_exception=True)\ndef _root(request):\n    template = get_template(f\"{name}.html\")\n    return HttpResponse(template.render({}, request))\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nfrom django.contrib.auth.decorators import login_required, permission_required\nfrom django.http import HttpResponse\nfrom django.template.loader import get_template\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Broken Access Control](https://owasp.org/www-community/Broken_Access_Control)\n- [OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-285: Improper Authorization](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/285.html)\n- [Django authentication system](https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/stable/topics/auth/default/)\n- [Django login_required decorator](https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/stable/topics/auth/default/#the-login-required-decorator)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-285",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/285.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-285"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "5940af87-3ad3-37b1-8387-68c65c942815",
              "name": "Direct Data Rendering Bypasses XSS Defenses",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThis is a cross-site scripting risk caused by returning HTML directly in a response instead of using a templating mechanism that escapes untrusted content. In Python web applications built with Django, sending a string through HttpResponse can expose users to script injection if any part of that string is influenced by request data, database content, or other external input.\n\nThe sink shown, return HttpResponse(template), bypasses Django's template auto-escaping protections when template contains rendered HTML content. The safer pattern is to pass data as context into a Django template and return the page with render(), so variables are escaped by default. If plain text is intended, return it with a text/plain content type rather than as HTML.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not construct HTML in Python code and send it directly with HttpResponse when the content can include untrusted data. Use Django's template engine with render(request, template_name, context) so auto-escaping is applied to variables by default. Keep untrusted input as data in the context instead of concatenating it into markup.\n\nIf the response is not meant to be HTML, set an appropriate non-HTML content type such as text/plain. Only mark content as safe in a template when it has been strictly sanitized for the exact output context. Avoid disabling auto-escaping globally or using raw HTML response bodies for dynamic pages.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom django.shortcuts import render\n\n\ndef my_view(request):\n    context = {\n        \"message\": request.GET.get(\"message\", \"\")\n    }\n    return render(request, \"my_view.html\", context)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nfrom django.shortcuts import render\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Cross Site Scripting (XSS)](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/xss/)\n- [OWASP Cross Site Scripting Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [Django render() shortcut](https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/stable/topics/http/shortcuts/#render)\n- [Django template language automatic HTML escaping](https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/stable/ref/templates/language/#automatic-html-escaping)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "5d7ffd57-ee5f-303e-aefa-f0ba35aaa3ea",
              "name": "Missing Function Level Access Control",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThis issue is a missing authentication and authorization check on a view function. In Python web applications, a request handler that can be reached without verifying the caller's identity and permissions allows unauthenticated or unauthorized users to invoke server-side behavior directly.\n\nIn the shown async view, the request is processed immediately, user-controlled input is read, a trigger function is resolved and executed, and a template is returned without any access-control enforcement. That means an attacker can reach sensitive code paths, invoke trigger behavior, and exercise special branches such as the raw output path unless the route is protected elsewhere. Access control should be enforced at the view boundary and, for sensitive actions, verified again before executing privileged functionality.\n\nThe fix is to require authentication on the view and perform an explicit permission check before resolving or calling the trigger. In Django, use the built-in authentication decorators or mixins and fail closed when the user is not authenticated or lacks the required permission. Keep authorization decisions close to the action being performed so route changes do not accidentally expose the function later.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nRequire authentication for every non-public view and enforce authorization before performing the protected action, not only at routing time. Use Django's built-in login and permission checks, return 403 for disallowed operations, and default to deny when a trigger or action is not explicitly permitted.\n\nAvoid exposing dynamic function dispatch to untrusted users unless each target is mapped through a strict allowlist and guarded by permission checks. If different triggers represent different privilege levels, validate the current user's role for each one before execution. Also review related routes and API endpoints to ensure the same access-control rules are applied consistently across synchronous and asynchronous views.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom django.contrib.auth.decorators import login_required, permission_required\nfrom django.http import HttpResponse, HttpResponseForbidden\nfrom django.utils.html import escape\n\n@login_required\n@permission_required('app.can_run_trigger', raise_exception=True)\nasync def _view(request):\n    user_input = await _get_user_input(request)\n\n    name = request.GET.get(\"name\", \"\")\n    trigger = request.GET.get(\"trigger\", \"\")\n\n    allowed_triggers = {\"safe\": \"safe\"}\n    if trigger not in allowed_triggers:\n        return HttpResponseForbidden(\"Forbidden\")\n\n    trigger_func = get_trigger(name, trigger)\n    if trigger_func is None:\n        return HttpResponseForbidden(\"Forbidden\")\n\n    trigger_func(user_input)\n\n    template = get_template(\"{}.html\".format(name))\n\n    if name == \"xss\" and trigger == \"raw\":\n        return HttpResponseForbidden(\"Forbidden\")\n\n    return HttpResponse(template + \"<p>XSS: \" + escape(user_input) + \"</p>\")\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\ndjango\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Broken Access Control](https://owasp.org/www-community/Broken_Access_Control)\n- [OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-306: Missing Authentication for Critical Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html)\n- [CWE-862: Missing Authorization](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html)\n- [Django authentication system](https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/stable/topics/auth/default/)\n- [Django view decorators](https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/stable/topics/http/decorators/)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-306",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-862",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-306",
                  "CWE-862"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "644b8010-960a-36da-9ca3-b6c5fda7a36a",
              "name": "Insecure XML Parsing With Untrusted Data",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnsafe XML parsing of untrusted input. In Python, xml.etree.ElementTree.fromstring() will parse attacker-controlled XML without the hardened protections needed against common XML parser attacks such as entity expansion and related denial-of-service conditions.\n\nIn the shown sink, ET.fromstring(xml) accepts raw XML data directly. When that data comes from a request, file upload, message queue, or other untrusted source, an attacker can supply malicious XML that consumes excessive CPU or memory during parsing. Replace the standard library parser call with the defusedxml equivalent so unsafe constructs are blocked by default.\n\nIf this code must continue using ElementTree APIs, parse untrusted XML through defusedxml.ElementTree.fromstring instead of xml.etree.ElementTree.fromstring. That keeps the calling pattern similar while applying safer parser behavior for hostile input.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not parse untrusted XML with the standard library XML parsers unless they have been explicitly hardened. Prefer defusedxml for any XML that originates outside the trust boundary, and keep parsing limits and error handling strict so malformed or malicious documents are rejected early. If the application does not require XML features such as entities or DTDs, use parsers that disable them by default or reject documents containing them.\n\nReview all XML entry points, not just this call site, including fromstring, parse, iterparse, minidom, sax, and lxml configurations. Apply the same safe parser choice consistently across the codebase, and treat XML parsing failures as security-relevant validation errors rather than retrying with a more permissive parser.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom defusedxml import ElementTree as ET\n\nnode = ET.fromstring(xml)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\ndefusedxml>=0.7.1\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XML Security Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/XML_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-611: Improper Restriction of XML External Entity Reference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/611.html)\n- [Python documentation: XML vulnerabilities](https://docs.python.org/3/library/xml.html#xml-vulnerabilities)\n- [defusedxml project documentation](https://pypi.org/project/defusedxml/)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-611",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/611.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-611"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "65d8f510-285e-3c34-b079-c11a947d763c",
              "name": "Subprocess Call With Shell=True Security Issue",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThis issue is an OS command injection risk. In Python, using subprocess.Popen with shell=True causes the command string to be interpreted by a shell, so any untrusted input included in that command can execute unintended shell syntax, additional commands, or file redirection.\n\nFor this sink, the safest fix is to avoid the shell entirely and pass the executable and arguments as a list. This makes Python invoke the target program directly instead of asking a shell to parse the command. If external input is involved, validate it against a strict allowlist before passing it as an argument.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not build shell command strings from variable input. Prefer subprocess.Popen, subprocess.run, or similar APIs with shell=False and provide arguments as a sequence so the operating system does not perform shell parsing. Use absolute executable paths where practical, restrict user input with an allowlist of expected characters or values, and separate validation from execution. If a shell is truly required, do not concatenate input into the command; redesign the call so untrusted data is handled as data, not shell syntax.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport subprocess\n\nuser_arg = get_user_input()\nif not user_arg.isalnum():\n    raise ValueError(\"Invalid argument\")\n\nprocess = subprocess.Popen(\n    [\"/usr/bin/example\", user_arg],\n    shell=False,\n    stdout=subprocess.PIPE,\n    stderr=subprocess.PIPE,\n    text=True,\n)\nstdout, stderr = process.communicate()\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nsubprocess (Python standard library)\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Command Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Command_Injection)\n- [CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)\n- [Python subprocess module documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/subprocess.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-78",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-78"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6d662a3d-95a8-3297-bd62-f0f9f10de45c",
              "name": "Insecure Deserialization Of Pickle Data",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnsafe deserialization. In Python, pickle.loads() reconstructs arbitrary Python objects and can execute attacker-controlled behavior during deserialization. When the input comes from a user-controlled value such as user_input, this creates a direct path to remote code execution, denial of service, or application state manipulation.\n\nThe sink shown converts text to bytes and immediately passes it to pickle.loads(). That is still dangerous because the risk is caused by unpickling untrusted bytes, not by the string-to-bytes conversion. For data received from users, replace pickle with a safe interchange format such as JSON and validate the decoded structure before using it.\n\nIf serialized Python objects must be accepted, only deserialize data from a trusted source protected by strong authentication and integrity checks, and avoid exposing such endpoints to untrusted clients. For this code path, the correct fix is to stop using pickle.loads() on user input and parse structured data with json.loads() instead.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not use pickle, dill, shelve, or similar Python object deserialization mechanisms on data that originates from users, requests, cookies, message queues, or other untrusted boundaries. Prefer JSON for simple structured data and explicitly validate the expected type, required keys, and value formats after parsing.\n\nIf you need stronger schema validation, validate the decoded JSON against a fixed schema before processing it. Keep deserialization logic narrow, reject unexpected fields, and enforce size limits on input to reduce denial-of-service risk. Where legacy pickle data cannot be removed immediately, restrict it to trusted internal producers and protect it with integrity verification and authenticated transport.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport json\n\n\ndef load_user_data(user_input: str):\n    data = json.loads(user_input)\n    if not isinstance(data, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"Expected a JSON object\")\n    return data\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nimport json\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [Python pickle documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/pickle.html)\n- [OWASP: Deserialization of untrusted data](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Deserialization_of_untrusted_data)\n- [CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html)\n- [Python json documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/json.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-502",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-502"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "735672a8-c394-3273-a946-a9099dd5dc5e",
              "name": "Direct Data Rendering Bypasses XSS Defenses",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThis is a cross-site scripting risk caused by returning HTML directly in a response instead of using a templating mechanism that escapes untrusted content. In Python web applications built with Django, sending a string through HttpResponse can expose users to script injection if any part of that string is influenced by request data, database content, or other external input.\n\nThe sink shown, return HttpResponse(template), bypasses Django's template auto-escaping protections when template contains rendered HTML content. The safer pattern is to pass data as context into a Django template and return the page with render(), so variables are escaped by default. If plain text is intended, return it with a text/plain content type rather than as HTML.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not construct HTML in Python code and send it directly with HttpResponse when the content can include untrusted data. Use Django's template engine with render(request, template_name, context) so auto-escaping is applied to variables by default. Keep untrusted input as data in the context instead of concatenating it into markup.\n\nIf the response is not meant to be HTML, set an appropriate non-HTML content type such as text/plain. Only mark content as safe in a template when it has been strictly sanitized for the exact output context. Avoid disabling auto-escaping globally or using raw HTML response bodies for dynamic pages.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom django.shortcuts import render\n\n\ndef my_view(request):\n    context = {\n        \"message\": request.GET.get(\"message\", \"\")\n    }\n    return render(request, \"my_view.html\", context)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nfrom django.shortcuts import render\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Cross Site Scripting (XSS)](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/xss/)\n- [OWASP Cross Site Scripting Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [Django render() shortcut](https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/stable/topics/http/shortcuts/#render)\n- [Django template language automatic HTML escaping](https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/stable/ref/templates/language/#automatic-html-escaping)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "79268c67-68ca-325e-863d-3a2dc123449a",
              "name": "Insecure Deserialization Of Pickle Data",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnsafe deserialization. In Python, pickle.load() reconstructs arbitrary Python objects and can execute attacker-controlled code during deserialization. When the data source is influenced by a user, calling pickle.load(user_input) allows crafted payloads to trigger code execution, data tampering, or denial of service.\n\nFor this sink, the fix is to stop using pickle for untrusted input and switch to a data-only format such as JSON. Parse the incoming stream with json.load() and validate the resulting structure before using it. If the application must handle complex internal Python objects, reserve pickle only for trusted, integrity-protected data that never originates from users or external clients.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not deserialize user-controlled bytes with pickle, dill, shelve, joblib, or other pickle-backed loaders. Prefer JSON or another schema-based serialization format for data crossing trust boundaries, and validate type, shape, and allowed fields after parsing. If a legacy design still requires pickle for internal use, ensure the serialized content is accepted only from fully trusted sources and is protected with strict access controls and integrity verification before loading.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport json\n\ndef load_user_data(user_input):\n    data = json.load(user_input)\n\n    if not isinstance(data, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"Expected a JSON object\")\n\n    allowed_keys = {\"id\", \"name\", \"email\"}\n    if not set(data.keys()).issubset(allowed_keys):\n        raise ValueError(\"Unexpected fields in input\")\n\n    if \"id\" in data and not isinstance(data[\"id\"], int):\n        raise ValueError(\"Field 'id' must be an integer\")\n    if \"name\" in data and not isinstance(data[\"name\"], str):\n        raise ValueError(\"Field 'name' must be a string\")\n    if \"email\" in data and not isinstance(data[\"email\"], str):\n        raise ValueError(\"Field 'email' must be a string\")\n\n    return data\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\njson (Python standard library)\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Deserialization_of_untrusted_data)\n- [CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html)\n- [Python Documentation: pickle](https://docs.python.org/3/library/pickle.html)\n- [Python Documentation: json](https://docs.python.org/3/library/json.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-502",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-502"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "7a57629a-aea7-36d1-b1b5-a8cdfc47f885",
              "name": "Missing Function Level Access Control",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThis is a missing function-level access control issue. The aiohttp view can be reached without enforcing authentication or authorization before executing sensitive behavior.\n\nIn this handler, request data is accepted, a trigger function is resolved dynamically, and then invoked. Without an explicit identity check and a permission check, any caller who can reach the route may be able to execute server-side actions and retrieve rendered content that should be restricted.\n\nIn Python web applications, access control should be enforced at the start of the handler or through middleware/decorators that run before business logic. For this sink, require an authenticated user on the request, verify that the user is allowed to invoke the selected action, and deny access before calling the trigger function or rendering the response.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nProtect the route with a centralized authentication mechanism and perform authorization checks for every request before any action is executed. Enforce a deny-by-default policy, map permissions to specific operations such as the selected name and trigger, and return 401 for unauthenticated callers and 403 for authenticated callers without permission.\n\nAvoid relying on hidden routes, client-side controls, or naming conventions as access control. Prefer middleware or reusable decorators so checks are applied consistently across handlers. Keep sensitive operations off dynamic dispatch paths unless the requested action is validated against an explicit allowlist and the current user is authorized for that exact operation.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom aiohttp import web\n\n\ndef get_current_user(request):\n    user = request.get(\"user\")\n    if user is None:\n        raise web.HTTPUnauthorized(text=\"Authentication required\")\n    return user\n\n\ndef can_invoke_trigger(user, name, trigger):\n    allowed = user.get(\"allowed_triggers\", set())\n    return (name, trigger) in allowed\n\n\nasync def _view(request):\n    user = get_current_user(request)\n\n    name = request.match_info.get(\"name\", \"\")\n    trigger = request.match_info.get(\"trigger\", \"\")\n\n    if not can_invoke_trigger(user, name, trigger):\n        raise web.HTTPForbidden(text=\"Forbidden\")\n\n    user_input = _get_user_input(request)\n    trigger_func = get_trigger(name, trigger)\n\n    if trigger_func is None:\n        raise web.HTTPNotFound(text=\"Unknown action\")\n\n    trigger_func(user_input)\n\n    template = get_template(\"{}.html\".format(name))\n    return web.Response(text=template, content_type=\"text/html\")\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\naiohttp\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Broken Access Control](https://owasp.org/www-community/Broken_Access_Control)\n- [OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-862: Missing Authorization](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html)\n- [aiohttp Web Server Quickstart](https://docs.aiohttp.org/en/stable/web_quickstart.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-862",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-862"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "7f18b2d5-9d33-36bc-97ae-4aec5ba666e8",
              "name": "Insecure Deserialization of Untrusted Data",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnsafe deserialization. In Python, using pickle.loads on data influenced by a user is dangerous because pickle can recreate arbitrary objects and execute code during deserialization. That turns a simple data-parsing operation into a potential remote code execution path.\n\nIn this sink, user_input is encoded and passed directly to pickle.loads. Because the bytes come from an untrusted source, an attacker can supply a crafted payload that triggers malicious behavior as soon as it is deserialized. The fix is to stop using pickle for untrusted input and switch to a data-only format such as JSON, then validate the parsed structure before returning or using it.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not deserialize untrusted input with pickle, marshal, or shelve. Use a safe serialization format such as JSON for externally supplied data, and validate the decoded value against the expected type, required fields, and allowed ranges before processing it. If object reconstruction is required for trusted internal data, keep pickle strictly limited to authenticated, integrity-protected sources that are not attacker-controlled. Prefer simple data-transfer objects over executable object graphs.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport json\n\n\ndef parse_user_input(user_input: str):\n    data = json.loads(user_input)\n    if not isinstance(data, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"Expected a JSON object\")\n    return data\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nimport json\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Deserialization_of_untrusted_data)\n- [CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html)\n- [Python Documentation: pickle — Python object serialization](https://docs.python.org/3/library/pickle.html)\n- [Python Documentation: json — JSON encoder and decoder](https://docs.python.org/3/library/json.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-502",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-502"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "879ada17-1f55-367f-9351-21fdb24acb7e",
              "name": "Untrusted Input Allows Arbitrary Code Execution",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnsafe dynamic module loading. In Python, passing untrusted data into importlib.import_module() allows an attacker to influence which module is loaded and executed.\n\nThe sink shown as return import_module(module_name) is dangerous when module_name can be affected by request data, configuration controlled by a user, or any other untrusted source. Importing a module executes its top-level code and can expose unexpected objects or behavior. This can lead to arbitrary code execution, privilege misuse, or loading internal modules that were never intended to be reachable.\n\nThe fix is to stop importing arbitrary module names directly. Replace dynamic imports with a fixed allowlist that maps approved external values to known-safe module paths, then import only from that map. If the set of modules is static, prefer direct imports over runtime selection entirely.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not pass user-controlled strings into importlib.import_module(), __import__(), eval(), or similar runtime code-selection APIs. Define a strict allowlist of permitted choices and map external identifiers to constant internal module paths. Keep the allowlist small, reject unknown values explicitly, and prefer direct imports when the module set is known at development time. If plugins are required, load them only from trusted packages or signed deployment artifacts and keep discovery separate from untrusted input handling.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom importlib import import_module\n\n_ALLOWED_MODULES = {\n    \"json\": \"json\",\n    \"csv\": \"csv\",\n    \"xml\": \"xml.etree.ElementTree\",\n}\n\ndef load_module(module_name: str):\n    try:\n        safe_module = _ALLOWED_MODULES[module_name]\n    except KeyError as exc:\n        raise ValueError(\"Unsupported module selection\") from exc\n\n    return import_module(safe_module)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nfrom importlib import import_module\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Unsafe Use of Reflection](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Unsafe_use_of_Reflection)\n- [CWE-470: Use of Externally-Controlled Input to Select Classes or Code](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/470.html)\n- [Python Documentation: importlib.import_module](https://docs.python.org/3/library/importlib.html#importlib.import_module)\n- [OWASP Input Validation Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Input_Validation_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-470",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/470.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-470"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "8f69c26e-7711-3f75-90bf-2954da075ed7",
              "name": "Insecure XML Parsing With Untrusted Data",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThis is an unsafe XML parsing issue. Parsing untrusted XML with a parser that allows dangerous XML features can expose the application to XML External Entity attacks, entity expansion, and denial-of-service conditions.\n\nIn Python, calling lxml.etree.parse on attacker-controlled XML can process DTDs and external entities unless the parser is explicitly hardened. In the shown sink, XML is parsed directly from StringIO(xml), so malicious input may trigger local file access, server-side requests, or resource exhaustion.\n\nMitigate this by using a hardened XML parser for untrusted input. For this sink, replace direct lxml parsing with the defusedxml equivalent so hostile XML features are blocked by default before the document tree is built.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nUse hardened XML parsers whenever XML comes from users, requests, files, queues, or other untrusted sources. Disable DTD processing, external entity resolution, and network access if a hardened wrapper is not used. Apply size and complexity limits to XML inputs and prefer simpler data formats such as JSON when full XML features are not required.\n\nIf lxml must remain in use for compatibility reasons, construct an XMLParser with resolve_entities=False, no_network=True, and load_dtd=False, and avoid enabling options that reintroduce entity expansion or external resource loading. Keep XML-related libraries updated and review all parsing entry points, not just this one.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom io import StringIO\nfrom defusedxml.lxml import parse\n\nxml_stream = StringIO(xml)\ntree = parse(xml_stream)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\ndefusedxml\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: XML External Entity Processing](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/XML_External_Entity_(XXE)_Processing)\n- [CWE-611: Improper Restriction of XML External Entity Reference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/611.html)\n- [defusedxml on PyPI](https://pypi.org/project/defusedxml/)\n- [lxml Parsing XML and HTML with lxml](https://lxml.de/parsing.html)\n- [Python Documentation: XML security vulnerabilities](https://docs.python.org/3/library/xml.html#xml-vulnerabilities)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-611",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/611.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-611"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "917d64cb-c673-3bf6-ba2c-e13de68da33b",
              "name": "Missing Function Level Access Control",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nMissing function-level access control. This Python view function returns application content directly and does not enforce authentication or authorization before serving the response. If the route is exposed, any caller who can reach it may access the page regardless of whether the user is logged in or permitted to view it.\n\nIn Django-style applications, access checks should be applied at the view boundary so every request is validated before business logic or template rendering runs. For a handler such as `_root(request)`, require login at minimum and, when the page is not public, enforce an explicit permission check tied to the resource or action being accessed. This prevents accidental exposure caused by unsecured routes or future code changes.\n\nBecause this function renders a template response, the safest fix is to protect the endpoint with built-in authentication and permission decorators. That keeps the control close to the entry point and makes the expected access policy obvious in code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nProtect every non-public view with server-side authentication and authorization checks at the route or view layer. Prefer framework-provided decorators or mixins such as `login_required`, `permission_required`, or class-based equivalents so the policy is enforced consistently before the handler executes.\n\nDo not rely on hidden links, client-side checks, or template logic to restrict access. Define explicit permissions for sensitive pages and verify them for each request. If access depends on the specific object being viewed, perform object-level authorization inside the view after confirming the user is authenticated.\n\nReview URL mappings for similar handlers that return templates or data without guards, and adopt a secure default posture where routes require authentication unless they are intentionally public. Add automated tests that verify anonymous users are redirected or denied and unauthorized users receive a forbidden response.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom django.contrib.auth.decorators import login_required, permission_required\nfrom django.http import HttpResponseForbidden\nfrom django.shortcuts import render\n\n@login_required\n@permission_required('app.view_root_page', raise_exception=True)\ndef _root(request):\n    return render(request, f\"{name}.html\")\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\ndjango\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10: Broken Access Control](https://owasp.org/Top10/A01_2021-Broken_Access_Control/)\n- [OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Django Security Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Django_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [Django authentication system](https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/stable/topics/auth/default/)\n- [CWE-306: Missing Authentication for Critical Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html)\n- [CWE-862: Missing Authorization](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-306",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-862",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-306",
                  "CWE-862"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "971fb3d3-2e80-39f1-a029-be7c5b5ed5ef",
              "name": "Insecure YAML Deserialization Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnsafe YAML deserialization can occur when Python code parses attacker-controlled YAML with a loader that supports arbitrary Python object construction. In this case, `yaml.load(user_input, Loader=yaml.UnsafeLoader)` allows specially crafted input to instantiate objects or trigger code execution during deserialization.\n\nIf `user_input` can be influenced by an external caller, this creates a direct path to remote code execution and full process compromise. The fix is to replace the unsafe loader with `yaml.safe_load` or `yaml.load(..., Loader=yaml.SafeLoader)` so only standard YAML types are accepted.\n\nFor this sink, the safest remediation is to parse the input with `yaml.safe_load`, then validate that the resulting data matches the expected Python types and structure before using it. If the application requires custom object deserialization, do not accept untrusted YAML for that code path.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not use `yaml.unsafe_load`, `yaml.UnsafeLoader`, `yaml.Loader`, or `yaml.CLoader` on untrusted input. Parse untrusted YAML with `yaml.safe_load` or `yaml.load(..., Loader=yaml.SafeLoader)` and enforce strict validation on the resulting object graph, such as checking expected container types, required keys, and scalar value formats. If only simple configuration data is needed, consider limiting accepted content to dictionaries, lists, strings, numbers, booleans, and null values. Keep PyYAML updated and isolate any legacy code that still requires unsafe deserialization so it never processes externally controlled input.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport yaml\n\n\ndef parse_user_yaml(user_input: str):\n    data = yaml.safe_load(user_input)\n    if not isinstance(data, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"Expected a YAML mapping\")\n    return data\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nPyYAML\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Deserialization_of_untrusted_data)\n- [CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html)\n- [PyYAML Documentation](https://pyyaml.org/wiki/PyYAMLDocumentation)\n- [Unsafe YAML load in PyYAML](https://sourcery.ai/vulnerabilities/python-pyyaml-unsafe-load-rce)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-502",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-502"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "9eb7c8b7-70e3-397f-91b1-7cc667f067f8",
              "name": "Missing Function Level Access Control",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nMissing function-level access control. This Python view function is directly reachable and performs a redirect without any authentication or authorization checks, so any caller who can access the route may invoke it. In a web application, leaving a view unprotected can expose internal pages, workflow entry points, or sensitive actions to unauthenticated users.\n\nFor a Pyramid-style view like the one shown, access control should be enforced at the view or route level before returning the redirect. Require an authenticated principal and, if the target is intended only for certain users, apply a permission check as well. This ensures the framework denies unauthorized requests consistently instead of relying on callers or downstream views to protect access.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nProtect every externally reachable view with explicit authentication and authorization rules. In Python web applications, prefer framework-supported access control such as Pyramid view permissions, security policies, and forbidden handling rather than ad hoc checks scattered through business logic.\n\nApply least privilege to each route, require login before redirecting users into protected areas, and verify permissions for role-restricted endpoints. Avoid assuming that a redirect target is safe just because the destination performs its own checks; the entry-point view should still enforce policy. Add tests that confirm anonymous users receive denial or redirection to login and that authenticated users without the required permission are blocked.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom pyramid.httpexceptions import HTTPFound\nfrom pyramid.security import NO_PERMISSION_REQUIRED\nfrom pyramid.view import view_config\n\n@view_config(route_name=\"index\", permission=\"view\")\ndef index(request):\n    if request.authenticated_userid is None:\n        raise HTTPFound(location=request.route_url(\"login\"))\n    return HTTPFound(location=\"/vulnpy\")\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\npyramid\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Broken Access Control](https://owasp.org/www-community/Broken_Access_Control)\n- [OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Authentication Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authentication_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-306: Missing Authentication for Critical Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html)\n- [CWE-862: Missing Authorization](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html)\n- [Pyramid Security Documentation](https://docs.pylonsproject.org/projects/pyramid/en/latest/narr/security.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-306",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-862",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-306",
                  "CWE-862"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a68f8a51-3c30-3334-ac76-850ef44bcdf1",
              "name": "Formatted Sql Query",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nSQL injection in database query execution. Building a SQL statement with string formatting and passing it to cursor.execute(sql) allows untrusted input to change the query structure, which can expose, modify, or delete data.\n\nIn Python DB-API code, values must be bound separately from the SQL text. For this sink, keep the statement static and pass user-controlled data as parameters to cursor.execute(..., params). This ensures the database driver treats input as data instead of executable SQL and is the correct mitigation for this call pattern.\n\nIf any dynamic part of the query is not a value placeholder, such as a table name, column name, sort direction, or SQL fragment, do not concatenate raw input. Restrict those choices with an explicit allowlist and select from predefined SQL fragments before executing the statement.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nUse parameterized queries for every variable value passed to SQL. In Python, follow the placeholder style required by the specific database driver and pass parameters as the second argument to cursor.execute. Do not use f-strings, percent formatting, str.format, or string concatenation to assemble SQL containing untrusted input. Validate and normalize input before binding it, and use strict allowlists for identifiers or query options that cannot be parameterized. Prefer least-privileged database accounts so that a successful injection has limited impact.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nuser_id = int(user_id)\nsql = \"SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = %s\"\ncursor.execute(sql, (user_id,))\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nNo additional dependencies required if using a Python DB-API 2.0 compatible database driver.\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP SQL Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/SQL_Injection)\n- [OWASP SQL Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/SQL_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-89: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an SQL Command](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html)\n- [Python sqlite3 documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/sqlite3.html)\n- [psycopg2 basic usage](https://www.psycopg.org/docs/usage.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-89",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-89"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a6c0f725-7a7c-384a-8971-38a555276301",
              "name": "Insecure Template Autoescape Configuration",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nImproper output encoding in server-side templates can lead to cross-site scripting. When a Jinja2 Environment is created without enabling autoescaping, HTML-special characters in untrusted data are rendered directly into the response instead of being escaped.\n\nIn Python, this is especially risky for templates that produce HTML, XML, or similar browser-consumed content. The shown sink creates an Environment with the default behavior, which does not automatically escape output. Configure the environment with autoescape enabled, preferably by using select_autoescape so escaping is applied to the appropriate template types while remaining explicit and maintainable.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nEnable autoescaping for any Jinja2 environment that renders HTML or XML. Prefer select_autoescape so escaping is tied to template extensions and string-based templates. Keep template data untrusted by default, avoid marking values safe unless they have been sanitized for the exact output context, and use context-appropriate encoding for JavaScript, CSS, URL, or attribute contexts instead of relying on HTML escaping alone.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom jinja2 import Environment, FileSystemLoader, select_autoescape\n\nloader = FileSystemLoader(\"templates\")\nenvironment = Environment(\n    loader=loader,\n    autoescape=select_autoescape(\n        enabled_extensions=(\"html\", \"htm\", \"xml\"),\n        default_for_string=True,\n        default=False,\n    ),\n)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\njinja2\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [Jinja2 Environment API](https://jinja.palletsprojects.com/en/stable/api/#jinja2.Environment)\n- [Jinja2 select_autoescape](https://jinja.palletsprojects.com/en/stable/api/#jinja2.select_autoescape)\n- [OWASP Cross Site Scripting (XSS)](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/xss/)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ab752e7e-8dec-3f8d-ad3f-2342ea5048b4",
              "name": "Insecure Function Usage - Consider Safer Alternatives",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThis is a dynamic code execution risk caused by evaluating untrusted input. In Python, calling eval() on data such as code allows the string to be interpreted as executable Python code, which can let an attacker run arbitrary expressions in the application process.\n\nIn the shown sink, return eval(code) executes whatever is present in code. If this value can be influenced by a user, request, file, message, or database field, an attacker can trigger code injection, data exposure, or denial of service. When the intent is to parse simple Python literals such as strings, numbers, lists, dicts, booleans, or None, replace eval() with ast.literal_eval(), which only accepts safe literal structures.\n\nIf the input is not meant to be a Python literal, do not evaluate it as code. Parse it with a format-specific parser such as json.loads() for JSON, or map expected commands to explicit functions instead of interpreting arbitrary source text.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not execute input as Python code. Replace eval() with a parser that matches the expected data format, such as ast.literal_eval() for Python literals or json.loads() for JSON. If only specific operations are allowed, implement an allowlist that maps known input values to predefined behavior rather than interpreting free-form expressions. Validate input shape and size before parsing, and reject unexpected content early. Avoid fallback paths that reintroduce eval(), exec(), compile(), or similar dynamic execution APIs.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport ast\n\ndef run_code(code):\n    return ast.literal_eval(code)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nimport ast\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Code Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Code_Injection)\n- [CWE-94: Improper Control of Generation of Code ('Code Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html)\n- [Python Documentation: eval()](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#eval)\n- [Python Documentation: ast.literal_eval()](https://docs.python.org/3/library/ast.html#ast.literal_eval)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-94",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-94"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c2381e64-4652-37de-847a-7576837b5c81",
              "name": "Arbitrary Code Execution Via Dynamic Execution",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nDynamic code compilation from untrusted input. In Python, passing attacker-controlled text to compile() turns data into executable code objects. If that code object is later executed, an attacker can run arbitrary Python statements with the privileges of the application.\n\nThe sink shown uses compile(code, \"vulnpy.py\", \"exec\"), which is specifically intended for compiling statement blocks, not simple literals. That makes it especially dangerous for tainted input because imports, file access, subprocess calls, and other harmful operations can be embedded in the supplied string. The safest fix is to stop compiling user input entirely and replace it with a non-executable representation such as structured data, a fixed dispatch table, or strict parsing of an allowed subset.\n\nIf the input is only meant to carry data, parse it with a safe format such as JSON instead of Python source. If limited expression handling is truly required, validate against a strict allowlist using Python's ast module and reject anything outside the expected node types before any evaluation step. Do not rely on compile() with restricted globals as a security boundary.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not use compile(), eval(), or exec() on any input that can be influenced by users, files, requests, environment variables, or external systems. Redesign the feature so input is treated as data, not Python source code. For command selection, use an explicit mapping of allowed actions to callable functions. For configuration or payloads, use JSON or another non-executable serialization format.\n\nWhen expression support is unavoidable, define a narrow grammar and enforce it before processing. In Python, parse with ast.parse and reject all nodes except a minimal allowlist appropriate for the business case. Keep the allowed surface small, reject statements entirely for cases like this exec-mode compile, and fail closed on validation errors. Avoid sandboxing claims based only on custom globals or locals because they do not reliably prevent code execution attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport json\n\ndef load_instructions(code: str):\n    return json.loads(code)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nimport json\nimport ast\npip install jsonschema\npip install pydantic\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Code Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Code_Injection)\n- [CWE-94: Improper Control of Generation of Code ('Code Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html)\n- [Python docs: compile()](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#compile)\n- [Python docs: ast module](https://docs.python.org/3/library/ast.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-94",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
