            {
              "id": "c5b49cf2-f7b0-376e-a2ff-97265467e8c3",
              "name": "Weak SHA1 Hash For Security",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nWeak cryptographic hash algorithm. In Python, hashlib.sha1() creates a SHA-1 digest, which is no longer appropriate for security-sensitive uses because collision attacks against SHA-1 are practical. If this digest is used for integrity protection, signature-related workflows, token derivation, or other trust decisions, an attacker may be able to craft different inputs that produce the same hash.\n\nFor the sink shown here, replace hashlib.sha1() with a stronger hash such as hashlib.sha256() or hashlib.sha512() when the value is used for security. If the hash is only needed for a non-security purpose such as legacy deduplication or partitioning, explicitly set usedforsecurity=False so the intent is clear and the runtime can enforce policy appropriately on supported Python versions.\n\nIn addition, if the code is storing passwords, a general-purpose hash is the wrong primitive even when upgraded from SHA-1. Use a dedicated password hashing function such as bcrypt, scrypt, or Argon2 instead of hashlib.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nUse SHA-256 or stronger for security-sensitive hashing in Python. Reserve SHA-1 only for compatibility cases that are explicitly non-security-related, and pass usedforsecurity=False when supported to document that choice. If the code hashes passwords, replace the hashing approach with a password-specific KDF such as bcrypt, scrypt, or Argon2. Also review any stored SHA-1 digests, signatures, or protocol usage and migrate them to stronger algorithms consistently across producers and consumers.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport hashlib\n\nh = hashlib.sha256()\nh.update(data)\ndigest = h.hexdigest()\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nhashlib (Python standard library)\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)\n- [Python hashlib documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/hashlib.html)\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [SHAttered: Practical collision attack against SHA-1](https://shattered.io/)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-327",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-327"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cb17dcd5-5c8d-3474-b836-fdc0e7c4a020",
              "name": "Starting A Process With A Shell",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nOS command injection in Python process execution. Using os.system(cmd) starts a shell to interpret the command string, so any untrusted data concatenated into cmd can change the command being run and execute unintended shell metacharacters, pipelines, redirections, or additional commands.\n\nIn Python, the safer pattern is to avoid the shell entirely and execute the target program with subprocess.run using a list of arguments and shell=False. This prevents shell parsing and keeps user input as data instead of executable syntax. If only a fixed operation is needed, validate input against a strict allowlist before passing it as an argument.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nReplace os.system with subprocess.run or another subprocess API that accepts an argument list, and keep shell=False. Do not build commands with string concatenation, interpolation, or format strings when any part may come from users, files, environment variables, or external systems. Prefer fixed executable paths and fixed flags.\n\nWhen input must influence behavior, restrict it to an allowlist of known-safe values or validate it against a narrow expected format before use. If invoking a shell is unavoidable, redesign the code if possible; otherwise apply strict input validation and escaping with extreme care, understanding that escaping is more error-prone than avoiding the shell entirely.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport subprocess\n\nuser_value = user_value.strip()\nallowed_values = {\"start\", \"stop\", \"status\"}\nif user_value not in allowed_values:\n    raise ValueError(\"invalid command option\")\n\nsubprocess.run([\"/usr/bin/mytool\", user_value], check=True, shell=False)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nsubprocess (Python standard library)\nshlex (Python standard library, only if parsing fixed trusted command fragments is required)\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Command Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Command_Injection)\n- [CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)\n- [Python subprocess — Subprocess management](https://docs.python.org/3/library/subprocess.html)\n- [Python shlex — Simple lexical analysis](https://docs.python.org/3/library/shlex.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-78",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-78"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cedba811-ad8d-3d44-b846-d20a242f5e32",
              "name": "Arbitrary Code Execution Via Dynamic Execution",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nDynamic code execution from untrusted input. In Python, passing attacker-controlled data to eval executes it as Python code, which can lead to arbitrary command execution, data access, or full process compromise.\n\nThe sink shown, return eval(code), is unsafe because any value that reaches code is treated as executable source. Replace eval with a non-executing parser when the intent is to read simple literals or structured data. If only Python literals are expected, use ast.literal_eval, which accepts strings, numbers, tuples, lists, dicts, booleans, and None without executing expressions.\n\nIf the input is not meant to be executable at all, validate it against a strict allowlist and parse it with a dedicated format parser such as json.loads instead of evaluating it. The fix should remove dynamic execution entirely for this code path.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not use eval, exec, or compile on data that can be influenced by a user, request, file, message, or database field. Treat incoming text as data, not code. For simple values, use ast.literal_eval. For structured payloads, prefer json.loads or another dedicated parser for the expected format.\n\nConstrain accepted input with strict validation before parsing, including type and size checks where appropriate. If expression evaluation is a business requirement, move to a purpose-built expression engine with a restricted grammar and no access to Python objects, attributes, imports, or function calls. Keep the allowed syntax minimal and explicit.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport ast\n\ndef parse_value(code: str):\n    return ast.literal_eval(code)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nimport ast\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Code Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Code_Injection)\n- [CWE-94: Improper Control of Generation of Code ('Code Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html)\n- [Python ast.literal_eval documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/ast.html#ast.literal_eval)\n- [Python eval() documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#eval)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-94",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-94"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d5153ce7-eb32-3e5a-aaa1-b6a2fa505ca3",
              "name": "Weak MD5 Hash For Security",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nWeak cryptographic hash algorithm usage. This code uses MD5 to digest user-controlled input and then hex-encodes the result. In Python, MD5 is considered broken for security-sensitive purposes because attackers can generate collisions and undermine integrity checks, signatures, token derivation, or password-related logic built on top of it.\n\nFor this sink, replace MD5 with a modern hash from hashlib such as SHA-256 when you need a general-purpose cryptographic digest. If the value is being used for password storage, do not use a fast hash at all; use a dedicated password hashing function instead. The md5(..., usedforsecurity=False) option is only appropriate for explicitly non-security use cases such as legacy deduplication, and should not be used to justify MD5 in security logic.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nUse hashlib.sha256, hashlib.sha384, or hashlib.sha512 for security-sensitive hashing in Python. If the input is text, encode it explicitly before hashing, for example user_input.encode(\"utf-8\"). For password storage, use a slow password hashing algorithm such as bcrypt, scrypt, or Argon2 instead of any direct digest from hashlib. Reserve hashlib.md5(..., usedforsecurity=False) only for clearly documented non-security workflows where collision resistance is irrelevant.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom binascii import hexlify\nfrom hashlib import sha256\n\ndigest = hexlify(sha256(user_input).digest()).decode(\"utf8\")\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nfrom binascii import hexlify\nfrom hashlib import sha256\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)\n- [Python hashlib documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/hashlib.html)\n- [RFC 6151: Updated Security Considerations for MD5 and HMAC-MD5](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc6151)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-327",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-327"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "e0ac4dd1-c669-3199-ae55-55983743fef3",
              "name": "Missing Function Level Access Control",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nMissing function-level access control. In Python web applications, a view or helper that processes request data without enforcing authentication and authorization can expose application behavior to anonymous or unauthorized users.\n\nThe shown sink reads user-controlled input from either request.GET or request.POST. If the surrounding view can be reached without login and permission checks, an attacker can invoke that code path directly and supply arbitrary values. This is especially risky when the returned input influences sensitive actions, data access, or downstream queries.\n\nRestrict access at the view entry point, not only inside helper logic. For Django-style request handling, require authenticated users with decorators such as login_required and enforce permission checks with permission_required or explicit request.user permission validation before reading or acting on user input.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nApply authentication and authorization checks to every view that handles request data or triggers business actions. Enforce access control before reading parameters and before performing any sensitive operation. In Django, prefer @login_required for authentication and @permission_required, user_passes_test, or explicit request.user.has_perm checks for authorization.\n\nDo not rely on helper functions like _get_user_input to provide security boundaries. Keep them side-effect free and place access control at the HTTP entry point where every request must pass. Use deny-by-default permissions, return 403 for unauthorized access, and review routes regularly to ensure newly added views are not exposed without decorators or equivalent middleware protections.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom django.contrib.auth.decorators import login_required, permission_required\nfrom django.http import HttpResponseForbidden\n\n\ndef _get_user_input(request):\n    if request.method == \"GET\":\n        return request.GET.get(\"user_input\", \"\")\n    return request.POST.get(\"user_input\", \"\")\n\n\n@login_required\n@permission_required(\"app.can_use_user_input\", raise_exception=True)\ndef my_view(request):\n    user_input = _get_user_input(request)\n    return process_user_input(user_input)\n\n\ndef guarded_view(request):\n    if not request.user.is_authenticated:\n        return HttpResponseForbidden(\"Authentication required\")\n    if not request.user.has_perm(\"app.can_use_user_input\"):\n        return HttpResponseForbidden(\"Not authorized\")\n\n    user_input = _get_user_input(request)\n    return process_user_input(user_input)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nDjango\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Broken Access Control](https://owasp.org/www-community/Broken_Access_Control)\n- [OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-306: Missing Authentication for Critical Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html)\n- [CWE-862: Missing Authorization](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html)\n- [Django authentication system](https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/stable/topics/auth/default/)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-306",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-862",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-306",
                  "CWE-862"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "e1ae6064-ebac-38b2-99de-bd8b8e0969e8",
              "name": "Missing Function Level Access Control",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThis is a broken access control issue: the view function is exposed without an authentication or authorization check before returning content. In Python web applications, any route bound directly to an unprotected handler can be invoked by unauthenticated callers unless access control is enforced in code or middleware.\n\nThe shown async view returns rendered HTML immediately from the request handler and does not verify the caller's identity, session, or permissions. If this endpoint is intended for authenticated users or a limited role, an attacker can request it directly and access functionality that should be restricted.\n\nProtect the handler by checking that the request is associated with an authenticated user and, where needed, that the user has the required role or permission before rendering the template. In aiohttp applications, this is commonly done with an authentication middleware plus an explicit guard in the handler that returns 401 for unauthenticated users and 403 for authenticated users lacking permission.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nEnforce authentication before the handler executes, preferably with centralized middleware that validates the session or bearer token and stores the authenticated user on the request. Do not rely on the route being obscure or linked only from trusted pages.\n\nApply authorization at the function level for every sensitive endpoint. Check roles or permissions explicitly in the handler or with a reusable decorator/helper, and return 401 when the caller is not logged in and 403 when the caller is authenticated but not allowed.\n\nUse a safe, fixed template name for this endpoint unless dynamic template selection is required and validated separately. Keep access control decisions server-side, log denied requests, and add tests that verify anonymous and low-privilege users cannot access protected views.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom aiohttp import web\nfrom jinja2 import Environment, FileSystemLoader, select_autoescape\n\nenv = Environment(\n    loader=FileSystemLoader(\"templates\"),\n    autoescape=select_autoescape([\"html\", \"xml\"]),\n)\n\n\ndef require_authenticated_user(request):\n    user = request.get(\"user\")\n    if user is None or not user.get(\"is_authenticated\", False):\n        raise web.HTTPUnauthorized(text=\"Authentication required\")\n    return user\n\n\ndef require_permission(user, permission):\n    permissions = set(user.get(\"permissions\", []))\n    if permission not in permissions:\n        raise web.HTTPForbidden(text=\"Insufficient permissions\")\n\n\nasync def _root_view(request):\n    user = require_authenticated_user(request)\n    require_permission(user, \"view_root\")\n\n    template = env.get_template(\"root.html\")\n    html = template.render(user=user)\n    return web.Response(text=html, content_type=\"text/html\")\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\naiohttp\njinja2\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10: Broken Access Control](https://owasp.org/Top10/A01_2021-Broken_Access_Control/)\n- [OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-306: Missing Authentication for Critical Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html)\n- [CWE-862: Missing Authorization](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-306",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-862",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-306",
                  "CWE-862"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "e4e927eb-c261-3472-8803-e184891e5b9a",
              "name": "Insecure YAML Deserialization Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnsafe YAML deserialization. In Python, using PyYAML with yaml.load_all(..., Loader=yaml.UnsafeLoader) allows YAML documents to instantiate arbitrary Python objects during parsing. If an attacker can influence user_input, they can craft payloads that execute code or trigger dangerous object construction while the YAML is being loaded.\n\nFor the sink shown, the risk comes from processing untrusted YAML with UnsafeLoader. When multiple documents are expected, the safe fix is to keep yaml.load_all but switch to yaml.SafeLoader, or use yaml.safe_load_all. This restricts parsing to standard YAML types such as dictionaries, lists, strings, numbers, and booleans, which prevents object deserialization and code execution paths.\n\nIf the application genuinely requires custom Python objects, do not deserialize directly from untrusted input. Define a strict schema for allowed fields, load only primitive data with a safe loader, and then map that data into application objects explicitly. This keeps parsing separate from object creation and avoids attacker-controlled behavior during deserialization.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nUse yaml.safe_load or yaml.safe_load_all for any YAML that can come from users, files, APIs, environment variables, or other untrusted sources. Avoid yaml.load, yaml.load_all, yaml.Loader, yaml.CLoader, and yaml.UnsafeLoader when parsing externally controlled data.\n\nAfter safe parsing, validate the resulting structure and types before using the data. If specific fields are expected, enforce an allowlist of keys and value types. When complex application objects are needed, construct them manually from validated primitive values instead of letting the YAML parser create objects implicitly.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport yaml\n\nresult = list(yaml.safe_load_all(user_input))\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nPyYAML>=6.0\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Deserialization_of_untrusted_data)\n- [CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html)\n- [PyYAML Documentation](https://pyyaml.org/wiki/PyYAMLDocumentation)\n- [Unsafe PyYAML load Remote Code Execution Overview](https://sourcery.ai/vulnerabilities/python-pyyaml-unsafe-load-rce)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-502",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-502"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "e71b572d-95da-3d5f-889e-9cb4b40de582",
              "name": "Insecure Deserialization of Untrusted Data",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnsafe deserialization can lead to arbitrary code execution. In Python, `pickle.load()` reconstructs Python objects by executing instructions embedded in the serialized stream, so passing attacker-controlled input to this sink allows malicious payloads to run code during deserialization.\n\nIn the shown sink, `pickle.load(user_input)` directly consumes untrusted data. This is not safe for data received from users, requests, cookies, files, or other external sources. Replace pickle-based deserialization with a data-only format such as JSON and validate the parsed structure before using it.\n\nIf the application must accept serialized state, only deserialize data produced by a trusted source and protect it with integrity checks such as an HMAC or digital signature before loading. For this sink, the preferred fix is to stop using `pickle.load()` on untrusted input and parse a constrained JSON schema instead.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not use pickle, marshal, or shelve to deserialize data from untrusted or user-controlled sources. Prefer data-only formats such as JSON and enforce strict type and field validation after parsing. Keep the accepted schema minimal and reject unexpected fields.\n\nWhen deserialization of complex objects is unavoidable, only accept data from a fully trusted source and verify integrity before processing, for example with a keyed HMAC or signature. Isolate the operation as much as possible, avoid implicit object construction, and treat every external serialized payload as hostile by default.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport json\n\ndef load_user_input(user_input):\n    data = json.load(user_input)\n\n    if not isinstance(data, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"Expected a JSON object\")\n\n    allowed_keys = {\"id\", \"name\", \"enabled\"}\n    if not set(data.keys()).issubset(allowed_keys):\n        raise ValueError(\"Unexpected fields in input\")\n\n    if \"id\" in data and not isinstance(data[\"id\"], int):\n        raise ValueError(\"Field 'id' must be an integer\")\n    if \"name\" in data and not isinstance(data[\"name\"], str):\n        raise ValueError(\"Field 'name' must be a string\")\n    if \"enabled\" in data and not isinstance(data[\"enabled\"], bool):\n        raise ValueError(\"Field 'enabled' must be a boolean\")\n\n    return data\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nimport json\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Deserialization_of_untrusted_data)\n- [CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html)\n- [Python Documentation: pickle](https://docs.python.org/3/library/pickle.html)\n- [Python Documentation: json](https://docs.python.org/3/library/json.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-502",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-502"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "eeba82c4-dcb8-3f2c-92b8-e7b576328a55",
              "name": "Weak SHA1 Hash For Security",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nWeak cryptographic hash algorithm. In Python, creating a digest with hashlib.new(\"SHA1\") selects SHA-1, which is no longer appropriate for security-sensitive uses because collision attacks are practical.\n\nIf this hash is used for integrity checks, signatures, token derivation, password handling, or any other security decision, an attacker can exploit SHA-1 weaknesses to produce different inputs with the same digest. For the sink shown, replace SHA-1 with a modern hash such as SHA-256 or SHA-512. If the code is only computing a non-security checksum for legacy compatibility, explicitly mark that intent with usedforsecurity=False on supported Python versions.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nUse SHA-256, SHA-384, or SHA-512 for general security-sensitive hashing in Python. Prefer the dedicated constructors such as hashlib.sha256() when possible for clarity. If the digest is part of keyed integrity protection, use hmac with a strong hash instead of a bare hash. Do not use fast general-purpose hashes for password storage; use hashlib.pbkdf2_hmac, bcrypt, scrypt, or Argon2.\n\nOnly keep SHA-1 where interoperability is unavoidable and the result is not used for security. In that limited case, make the intent explicit with hashlib.new(\"sha1\", usedforsecurity=False) if your runtime supports it, and document the compatibility requirement so the weak algorithm is not reused in new security-sensitive code.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport hashlib\n\nh = hashlib.new(\"sha256\")\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nimport hashlib\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)\n- [Python hashlib documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/hashlib.html)\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-327",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-327"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f0e82ca8-9b70-33ca-9cf6-4f51ed8a955c",
              "name": "Vulnerable XML Parsing With Untrusted Data",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnsafe XML parsing of untrusted input. In Python, calling xml.sax.parseString on attacker-controlled XML can expose the application to XML parser attacks such as external entity expansion, entity-based denial of service, and other unsafe parser behaviors.\n\nThe sink shown parses user_input directly with the standard SAX parser. For untrusted data, switch to the hardened parser provided by defusedxml so dangerous XML features are blocked by default. This keeps the SAX-style parsing flow while protecting the application from malicious payloads embedded in the XML document.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not parse untrusted XML with the standard library XML parsers unless they have been explicitly hardened. In Python, prefer the corresponding defusedxml parser for SAX, DOM, ElementTree, and related APIs when processing data from users, APIs, files, or network sources. Apply the hardened parser at every XML entry point, and reject unexpected document types or oversized payloads when possible.\n\nIf replacing imports everywhere is not immediately feasible, initialize defusedxml.defuse_stdlib() early in process startup so standard-library XML modules are protected consistently. Keep XML handling code simple, avoid enabling DTD or external entity features, and add tests with malicious XML samples to verify the parser rejects unsafe input.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom defusedxml import sax\nfrom xml.sax.handler import ContentHandler\n\ndef parse_user_xml(user_input: str):\n    return sax.parseString(user_input, ContentHandler())\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\ndefusedxml\nfrom xml.sax.handler import ContentHandler\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: XML External Entity (XXE) Processing](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/XML_External_Entity_(XXE)_Processing)\n- [CWE-611: Improper Restriction of XML External Entity Reference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/611.html)\n- [defusedxml on PyPI](https://pypi.org/project/defusedxml/)\n- [Python documentation: xml.sax](https://docs.python.org/3/library/xml.sax.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-611",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/611.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-611"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f2c2c748-1d70-3571-9c7f-077458523b83",
              "name": "Insecure XML Parsing With Untrusted Data",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnsafe XML parsing. Parsing untrusted XML with Python XML parsers can allow XML-based attacks such as external entity expansion, entity amplification, and file or network access during parsing.\n\nIn this code, etree.fromstring(user_input) accepts attacker-controlled XML directly. When untrusted data is parsed without hardened settings, an attacker can supply crafted XML that triggers denial of service, reads local files, or causes server-side requests depending on parser behavior and configuration.\n\nFor this sink, replace direct parsing with a hardened parser from defusedxml.lxml so dangerous XML features are blocked by default. Keep treating all incoming XML as untrusted, and reject documents that exceed expected size or structure before further processing.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nUse hardened XML parsers for any data that can be influenced by users, external systems, or uploaded files. In Python, prefer defusedxml wrappers instead of direct XML parsing APIs when parsing untrusted content.\n\nAlso enforce input size limits, expected document shape, and time or resource limits where possible. If XML features such as DTDs or external entities are not required, keep them disabled and avoid custom parser configurations that re-enable network access or entity resolution.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom defusedxml.lxml import fromstring\n\ndef parse_xml(user_input: str):\n    return fromstring(user_input)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\ndefusedxml>=0.7.1\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: XML External Entity (XXE) Processing](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/XML_External_Entity_(XXE)_Processing)\n- [CWE-611: Improper Restriction of XML External Entity Reference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/611.html)\n- [defusedxml on PyPI](https://pypi.org/project/defusedxml/)\n- [lxml Parsing XML and HTML with lxml](https://lxml.de/parsing.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-611",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/611.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-611"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f45791d9-3686-30da-8159-0abe1f3b671a",
              "name": "Jinja2 Environment Without Autoescape Enabled",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-site scripting through unsafe template rendering. In Python, creating a Jinja2 Environment without autoescaping causes template expressions such as {{ ... }} to render raw HTML by default. If application data includes user-controlled content, that content can be injected into the page and executed in the browser.\n\nThe sink shown constructs Environment(loader=loader) without enabling escaping. For HTML-generating templates, configure Jinja2 to autoescape HTML, XML, and similar template types so variables are encoded before output. This preserves normal template usage while preventing untrusted data from becoming active script or markup in rendered responses.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nEnable Jinja2 autoescaping on every environment that renders HTML or XML. Prefer select_autoescape(...) so escaping is applied based on template file extension and for string-based templates. Keep autoescape enabled by default and only mark specific values as safe after they have been validated and intentionally sanitized for HTML output.\n\nAvoid disabling autoescape globally to accommodate a small number of trusted fragments. Instead, isolate those cases and use Jinja2's safe-handling features sparingly. Review templates for direct rendering of request data, database content, headers, and other untrusted values, because these become XSS sinks when emitted without escaping.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom jinja2 import Environment, FileSystemLoader, select_autoescape\n\nloader = FileSystemLoader(\"templates\")\nenvironment = Environment(\n    loader=loader,\n    autoescape=select_autoescape(\n        enabled_extensions=(\"html\", \"htm\", \"xml\"),\n        default_for_string=True,\n        default=False,\n    ),\n)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\njinja2>=3.1.0\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Cross Site Scripting (XSS)](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/xss/)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)\n- [Jinja2 Environment API](https://jinja.palletsprojects.com/en/stable/api/#jinja2.Environment)\n- [Jinja2 select_autoescape documentation](https://jinja.palletsprojects.com/en/stable/api/#jinja2.select_autoescape)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f87ddd31-3772-367a-b44a-dbebfeecd741",
              "name": "Missing Function Level Access Control",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThis is a missing function-level access control issue. The route handler is exposed without an authentication or authorization check, so any caller who can reach the endpoint can trigger the redirect and access application behavior that should be limited to authenticated or authorized users.\n\nIn Python web applications, access control should be enforced before executing the handler body. For an aiohttp-style route like this one, the handler should verify that a user identity is present on the request and confirm that the user is permitted to use the endpoint before returning a response. Relying on the route being obscure or assuming upstream protection is not sufficient because direct requests to the handler can bypass those assumptions.\n\nApply authentication and authorization consistently to each protected route, or centralize the check in middleware and keep explicit role checks in the handler for sensitive functions. For this sink, require a logged-in user before redirecting, and reject unauthenticated callers with 401 or unauthorized callers with 403 instead of processing the request.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nProtect every non-public endpoint with server-side authentication and authorization checks. In aiohttp, enforce authentication in middleware or a reusable decorator, then perform role or permission checks close to the handler when the action is sensitive. Return 401 when no authenticated identity is present and 403 when the user is authenticated but not allowed to access the route.\n\nDo not depend on client-side controls, hidden URLs, or reverse-proxy assumptions for access control. Define which routes are public and make protected-by-default the standard for application handlers. Keep authorization decisions based on trusted server-side session data, tokens, or identity context, and validate them on every request.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom aiohttp import web\nfrom aiohttp_session import get_session\n\nroutes = web.RouteTableDef()\n\n\ndef require_role(role_name):\n    def decorator(handler):\n        async def wrapped(request):\n            session = await get_session(request)\n            user_id = session.get(\"user_id\")\n            roles = session.get(\"roles\", [])\n\n            if not user_id:\n                raise web.HTTPUnauthorized(text=\"Authentication required\")\n\n            if role_name not in roles:\n                raise web.HTTPForbidden(text=\"Insufficient permissions\")\n\n            request[\"user_id\"] = user_id\n            return await handler(request)\n        return wrapped\n    return decorator\n\n\n@routes.get(\"/\")\n@require_role(\"user\")\nasync def index(request):\n    raise web.HTTPFound(\"/vulnpy\")\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\naiohttp\naiohttp-session\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Broken Access Control](https://owasp.org/www-community/Broken_Access_Control)\n- [OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-306: Missing Authentication for Critical Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html)\n- [CWE-862: Missing Authorization](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html)\n- [aiohttp Advanced Usage](https://docs.aiohttp.org/en/stable/web_advanced.html)\n- [aiohttp Web Server Quickstart](https://docs.aiohttp.org/en/stable/web_quickstart.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-306",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-862",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-306",
                  "CWE-862"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "fb71f94a-6752-3d13-8ebb-d15edcf086a7",
              "name": "Subprocess Called With Shell=True Can Lead To Command Injection",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nOS command injection. In Python, passing a command string to the operating system with os.system executes it through a shell, so any untrusted data embedded in that string can change the command being run.\n\nFor this sink, the safest fix is to avoid shell execution entirely and invoke the target program directly with subprocess.run using a list of arguments and shell=False. This prevents shell metacharacters from being interpreted as additional commands. If the input is expected to select from a limited set of values, validate it against an allowlist before execution.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not use os.system with data that can be influenced by a user, request, file, environment variable, or external service. Replace shell-based command construction with subprocess.run or subprocess.Popen using an argument list, keep shell=False, and pass each argument as a separate element. Validate untrusted input with a strict allowlist of expected values, especially when it controls flags, subcommands, paths, or filenames. If shell usage is unavoidable, redesign the code to remove untrusted input from the command entirely rather than attempting to escape it.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport subprocess\n\ndef run_command(user_arg: str) -> int:\n    allowed = {\"status\", \"version\", \"help\"}\n    if user_arg not in allowed:\n        raise ValueError(\"invalid command argument\")\n\n    result = subprocess.run(\n        [\"/usr/bin/mytool\", user_arg],\n        check=True,\n        shell=False\n    )\n    return result.returncode\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nimport subprocess\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Command Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Command_Injection)\n- [CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)\n- [Python subprocess module documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/subprocess.html)\n- [Python shlex module documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/shlex.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-78",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-78"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "fd5efed1-3906-377f-8293-1683f694011e",
              "name": "Starting A Process With A Shell",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nOS command injection in Python process execution. Using os.system(cmd) starts a shell to interpret the command string, so any untrusted data concatenated into cmd can change the command being run and execute unintended shell metacharacters, pipelines, redirections, or additional commands.\n\nIn Python, the safer pattern is to avoid the shell entirely and execute the target program with subprocess.run using a list of arguments and shell=False. This prevents shell parsing and keeps user input as data instead of executable syntax. If only a fixed operation is needed, validate input against a strict allowlist before passing it as an argument.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nReplace os.system with subprocess.run or another subprocess API that accepts an argument list, and keep shell=False. Do not build commands with string concatenation, interpolation, or format strings when any part may come from users, files, environment variables, or external systems. Prefer fixed executable paths and fixed flags.\n\nWhen input must influence behavior, restrict it to an allowlist of known-safe values or validate it against a narrow expected format before use. If invoking a shell is unavoidable, redesign the code if possible; otherwise apply strict input validation and escaping with extreme care, understanding that escaping is more error-prone than avoiding the shell entirely.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport subprocess\n\nuser_value = user_value.strip()\nallowed_values = {\"start\", \"stop\", \"status\"}\nif user_value not in allowed_values:\n    raise ValueError(\"invalid command option\")\n\nsubprocess.run([\"/usr/bin/mytool\", user_value], check=True, shell=False)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nsubprocess (Python standard library)\nshlex (Python standard library, only if parsing fixed trusted command fragments is required)\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Command Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Command_Injection)\n- [CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)\n- [Python subprocess — Subprocess management](https://docs.python.org/3/library/subprocess.html)\n- [Python shlex — Simple lexical analysis](https://docs.python.org/3/library/shlex.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-78",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-78"
                ]
              }
            }
          ],
          "language": "en-US",
          "contents": [
            "localizedData",
            "nonLocalizedData"
          ],
          "isComprehensive": false
        }
      },
